# Every rule checks/roster.sqlc declares or checks/all.sqlc emits, with that rule's rows counted by
# verdict. One row per rule.
#
# The rows into their rule, and into their verdict
#   checks/all.sqlc is a bag of rows, each naming a rule and carrying a verdict: a function from
#   rows to rules, and under it a function from rows to verdicts. Counting the rows per rule and per
#   verdict is the fibre-size reading of both, the rows as balls and the roster's rules as named
#   boxes, which is why the roster drives: a declared rule no row names is a row here with every
#   count at zero, where a `GROUP BY` over checks/all.sqlc alone would have no row for it at all.
#   The outer join is full, so a ball outside the declared boxes is kept too: a rule a check names
#   and the roster does not is a row here with `declared` false.
#
# The boxes partition the rows
#   `answered` is a row with a verdict, and splits into `violated` and `passed`. `silent` is a row
#   that examined a filing and returned no verdict, which the check contract forbids. `vacuous` is
#   the roster's own row, the one a check emits when its population is empty, so a rule with
#   nothing to examine still says so. Every row is answered, silent or vacuous, and every answered
#   row violated or passed, each box counted by its own predicate; algebra/subject_boxes.sqlc holds
#   both sums, and holds the sum of `rows` against checks/all.sqlc counted directly.
#
# The three regions are the grouping key
#   The rows are grouped on both sides' keys, not on one key read from either side. A rule both
#   sides name is one group; a rule only the roster names, or only a check, is a group with the
#   other side NULL. So the outer join's three kinds of row are the three shapes of the key, and a
#   join condition that stopped matching splits every rule into a declared rule with no rows and an
#   undeclared one with all of them, where merging on one key would put them back together and
#   hide it.
#
# The roster is taken as it is written
#   checks/roster.sqlc is keyed on the rule, and a second row naming one would double that rule's
#   counts here. Deduplicating it would hide exactly that, so it is joined as it stands and the sum
#   against checks/all.sqlc is what disagrees.
#
# One reading among several
#   folds/contract_subjects.sqlc unites this with the other six contracts, and says what each of
#   its readers asks of the same counts.
-- checks/roster.sqlc against checks/all.sqlc, one row per rule.
SELECT coalesce(r.rule, c.rule)                                                 AS subject,
       r.rule IS NOT NULL                                                       AS declared,
       count(c.rule)                                                            AS rows,
       count(c.rule) FILTER (WHERE c.violates IS NOT NULL)                      AS answered,
       count(c.rule) FILTER (WHERE c.violates)                                  AS violated,
       count(c.rule) FILTER (WHERE NOT c.violates)                              AS passed,
       count(c.rule) FILTER (WHERE c.violates IS NULL AND c.filing IS NOT NULL) AS silent,
       count(c.rule) FILTER (WHERE c.violates IS NULL AND c.filing IS NULL)     AS vacuous
FROM      (
    :compose(checks/roster.sqlc)
) r
FULL JOIN (
    :compose(checks/all.sqlc)
) c ON c.rule = r.rule
GROUP BY r.rule, c.rule
